o of  course as a result it disproportionately  benefits homes in the southeast how can  that be fair well I don't really think  that's right we we had direct our flood  defense spending into areas that are at  greatest risk and where the money will  protect the greatest number of  that does mean that some of the big  projects tend to be in our urban areas  but it's important to note that around  25% of all our flood spending has gone  to Yorkshire that's in recognition of  the fact the Yorkshire is at risk of  flooding and indeed last weekend I  visited York and I saw the Faust barrier  and there's been some significant  investment in that in order to improve  its capacity in the last five years so I  don't accept that we're not investing in  other areas but there's always more that  we want to do and that's why in the next  five years we're going to be spending  even more on flood defences and  crucially as well looking at nature  based solutions so moving upstream to  try to slow the flow of water down to  our urban areas in the first place using  dams and trees and natural floodplains  and the like so just to clarify you're  saying that is incorrect to say that  property prices help to determine the  flood defense formulas funding well my  understanding is the formula that is  applied by the Environment Agency is  looking at the number of homes that can  be protected from a given scheme and  that is why it is understandable that  your heart defenses in urban areas will  tend to be in your larger centers of  population because an investment there  will protect the greatest number of  homes but it's not universally the case  and Yorkshire has been a big recipient  of flood defence spending York alone  we've been spending around 70 million  pounds to provide protections in the  city of York and indeed upstream you can  see why though some people outside of  London would feel that if this was  happening in London in some of these big  cities closer to the politicians in  Westminster more would be done I mean  where is the Prime Minister this week  well look when I was a pointed a week  ago on Thursday the prime minister when  he met me the first thing he said is  there are some storms coming up storm  dennis is coming this weekend we know  the impacts are going to be  what action we would take it was agreed  that I would make a visit to look at uh  preparedness over that weekend it's not  true that the Prime Minister's had not  been engaged in this from the very  moment he appointed me he's been engaged  we stood up a National Flood Response  Center and they've been daily conference  calls that I've led and you know in a  cabinet government it's not a one-man  show it's right there on certain  operational things such as this the  Prime Minister will ask one of his  cabinet members to lead I can't see  anything wrong with that  I don't think anyone's disputing the  fact that it would be a you know a  one-man show but at the same time Boris  Johnson hasn't been seen in public for  nine days when there were floods back in  November he visited the affected areas  he called an emergency committee for the  government's Cobra committee why do you  think it is that he would do those  things during an election campaign and  not now well what happened on that  particular incident in the election is  because it was an election and there  were no ministers and we were in what's  called a a perder period where there was  less ministerial involvement in the  department there was seem to be  something of a slow start in a response  to that particular incident and that's  why because of the criticism the prime  minister in that instance did stand up  Cobra I think that's not being necessary  this time because we actually have sadly  because of the regular occurrence of  flood events we have something called  the national flood response center  hosted in the Cabinet coordinates across  government coordination and action on  these things that's been stood up over  ten days now and has been leading all of  the response to this so you didn't need  to stand up a separate Cabinet Office  infrastructure in the form of Cobra  because you already had one dedicated to  floods that was operating and you began  the interview talking about how extreme  weather is going to become more of a  frequent event connect government really  do anything to stop people's homes being  flooded or is this just an inevitability  of the world we're in  well it's I think inevitable that we're  going to get more of these extreme  weather events of course we have to do  our bit to try to tackle climate change  and as a country we are we've led it's  flooding in amphoteric we've led the  world  well flooding it's the frequency of it  these weather events of course is  inevitable because we've already got  climate change it's important that we do  our bit to mitigate that by leading the  world as we are in terms of reducing our  carbon emissions and decarbonizing our  economy and we're one of the first we  see the economies to actually commit to  net zero by 2050 but when it comes to  flooding there is more we want to do and  that is why we've committed to spend  another four billion pounds over the  next five years particularly looking at  some of those nature based solutions  where we can slow down the flow of water  hold water upstream use soft dams have  natural flood plains so that the water  is less likely to get to our urban  centers in the first place but we'll  never be able to protect every single  home it's important that we recognize  that but what we can do is do more and  more to protect as many homes as  possible okay  now the UK is going to this week publish  its negotiating mandate with the EU for  those upcoming trade talks the negotiate  negotiating bandit with the US we're  expecting the week after now your  predecessor in the job Tereza video said  last month that we will not be importing  chlorinated chicken we will not be  importing hormone treated beef will you  make the same commitment  [Music]  well look the truth is that it's already  illegal in this country to say it to  sell chlorine wash chicky and nor indeed  hormone beef that's in our legislation  but the important thing I would say is  we believe very passionately in this  country about food standards and our  animal welfare standards we worked very  hard over the last 20 years to build  quite a sophisticated market where  there's a lot of consumer confidence in  the provenance of our food and how it  was produced and the safety of our food  and we're absolutely clear as a  government that we will not take risks  either with our food standards and that  when it comes to animal welfare we will  be projecting our views on animal  welfare on the international stage it's  the UK that's been a world leader in  animal welfare particularly farmed  animal welfare and we want to bring the  rest of the world along with us so you  send that answer that it's currently  illegal to have chlorinated treatment a  chlorinated chicken or hormone treated  beef but of course when you've got a big  majority you can change that if you  wanted to so can you give a commitment  that even if the u.s. demanded it in a  trade deal it's a red line that we  wouldn't cross well I'm not quite sure  why the US would make such demands  because actually chlorine washes on  chicken are a very outdated technology  and it's not really used by the US  anymore in a way what they tend to use  these days are lactic acid washes and we  need even in this country we'll use well  what I'm saying is we won't make any  moves on our standards we've got a clear  position in this country that it is  illegal to sell chlorine wash chicken  illegal to sell beef treated with  hormones with no plans to change those  things but equally as I would say it's  not the case that the u.s. currently  used chlorine was chicken anyway okay  and now moving on to immigration because  we've got a bit more guidance on what  the post 4x8 immigration policy will  look like for this government and it's  all about restricting what the  government determines to be kind of  low-skilled workers of course one of the  sectors that relies heavily  workers from abroad is agriculture and  if you restrict the number of workers  that comes for abroad it's not going to  hit farmers and basically force people  into buying more food from overseas well  the government recognizes that  agriculture is a special case and that's  why what we've also announced in the  past week is that we're going to  quadruple the size of the seasonal  agricultural workers scheme to ten  thousand initially this year it's very  important that certain sectors such as  soft root vegetables and salad  production  they're quite reliant on seasonal labor  that's people who come here to work  maybe for six months before returning  home and we recognize that's entirely  different to people migrating to the UK  to settle permanently that's why a  seasonal agricultural worker scheme is  important and will be an important part  of our immigration policy in the future  so you mentioned that you're quadrupling  the number but that only takes the total  to 10,000 I mean the National Farmers  Union says we need 70,000 workers every  year it's nowhere near enough was it  well this was two half thousand last  year this is a second year of a pilot  and we've increased it to ten thousand  and of course the completion of that  pilot it's not this year no but they  don't need seventy thousand this year  because we still have free movement for  the remainder of 2020 and they will be  able to recruit the other staff that  they need from countries such as Romania  and in particular Bulgaria as they do as  they have done in previous years the  important thing will be in the future  and obviously the pilot will end this  year and will be in a position to roll  out a fully fledged seasonal  agricultural workers scheme that can be  designed to provide the needs of the  agricultural and horticultural sector  you see the government says it wants to  bring down low skilled immigration many  people would see perhaps a more accurate  description of that as low paid jobs  rather than low skilled jobs why is it  that high skilled jobs are highly paid  jobs should go to people from overseas  whereas the low paid ones who reserve  for bread  well I think the important thing to  recognize here is we want as a country  to do strike a different course on our  immigration policy rather than having  what we've had in recent years which is  effectively complete free movement with  EU countries and then quite a restricted  regime for the rest of the world it's  important that we are consistent and  that we have an approach based on skills  and that we enable people to come here  from wherever they might live if they  have the right skills to contribute to  our economy so identifying those areas  where we have a skill shortage whether  it might be vets whether it might be NHS  staff whether it might even be chefs or  people who do specialist welding  identifying those types of skills where  we have a gap and allowing people to  come to work in those sectors is a  logical approach rather than having free  movement and a complete free-for-all  with the European Union I mean the Home  Secretary said that they could have you  know a skilled jobs shortages could be  filled by the 8.5 million people classed  as economically inactive but according  to the Office of National Statistics  most of those people who are  economically inactive students carers  they've got long term illnesses or  they're retired yes I don't think the  Home Secretary was saying that all 88  million of those people would be able to  fill these just sir there are people  there who might and I think that's right  there will be some people who perhaps  retired but would like to still take a  part-time job there are students 